slml 0.2
use"layout.slml"

doc{$title"Lists, code and images"
  t.lead"The building blocks of real content: lists, code samples, images and dividers."
  section{$id"lists" $title"Lists"
    demo{$code"list {\n  item \"Bullets are the default\"\n  item { \"Items can contain \" bold \"inline\" \" elements\" }\n  list(marker: number) {\n    item \"A nested list follows an item\"\n    item \"and is indented under it\"\n  }\n}"
      -{
        "Bullets are the default"
        "Items can contain *inline* elements"
        +{"A nested list follows an item" "and is indented under it"}
      }
    }
    c{g0
      ref{$name"marker" $values"bullet / number / none" $about"The symbol before each item. Numbers count 1, 2, 3 …"}
      ref{$name"gap" $values"number" $about"Space between items (default 6)."}
    }
    "A list contains only `item`s and nested `list`s; an `item` can only be inside a list."
  }
  section{$id"code" $title"Code blocks"
    "`codeblock` shows text in a monospaced font on a light background, keeping every space. It is ideal with a [block string](/syntax.slml#strings):"
    demo{$code"codeblock \"\"\"\n    fn main() {\n        println!(\"Hello\");\n    }\n    \"\"\""
      code"""
          fn main() {
              println!("Hello");
          }
          """
    }
    "For a short piece of code inside a sentence use the inline `code` element."
  }
  section{$id"images" $title"Images"
    demo{$code"row(gap: 16, align: center) {\n  image(src: \"/logo.png\", alt: \"SLML logo\", width: 64, radius: 14)\n  image(src: \"/logo.png\", alt: \"Small SLML logo\", width: 32)\n  image(src: \"missing.png\", alt: \"This image is missing\", width: 160, height: 64)\n}"
      r{g16 a:c
        img{/logo.png w64 r14 "SLML logo"}
        img{/logo.png w32 "Small SLML logo"}
        img{./missing.png w160 h64 "This image is missing"}
      }
    }
    -{g6
      "`src` is a path to a PNG or JPEG file (or an https address). `alt` describes the image for people who can't see it, and it is *required*."
      "Purely decorative images say so with `alt: \"\"`."
      "Without `width` and `height`, an image has its natural size but never overflows its container. Give one of them and the other follows the image's proportions."
      "`radius`, `border`, `padding` and `shadow` work on images too, and `to` turns an image into a link."
    }
  }
  section{$id"dividers" $title"Dividers and spacers"
    demo{$code"text \"Above\"\ndivider\ntext \"Below\"\nrow(height: 40) { text \"Left\" divider(color: #2457d6, thickness: 2) text \"Right\" }"
      "Above"
      |
      "Below"
      r{h40 g8
        "Left"
        |{c#2457d6 th2}
        "Right"
      }
    }
    "In a row a divider is vertical. A `spacer` is empty space: without a size it takes all free space, with `height: 24` it is a fixed gap."
  }
  pager{$prev"/styling.slml" $prev-label"Boxes and styles" $next"/links.slml" $next-label"Links and navigation"}
}
